__d(function(e,a,i,t,n,o,r){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Part I (Civics)",chapter_number:27,chapter_title:"Urban Administration",estimated_study_time_minutes:45,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Understand what urban local bodies are and why cities need a separate system of governance","Identify the different types of urban local bodies in India: Municipal Corporation, Municipal Council, Town Panchayat","Describe the structure and functions of a Municipal Corporation","Explain the role of elected councillors, the Mayor, and the municipal commissioner","Understand how urban local bodies are funded and what services they provide","Identify the role of ward committees and how citizens can participate in urban governance","Connect urban administration to Karnataka-specific institutions like BBMP (Bruhat Bengaluru Mahanagara Palike)","Use correct vocabulary: ward, councillor, Mayor, municipal commissioner, property tax, BBMP"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Understanding of rural local self-government from Chapter 25 (Panchayati Raj)","Awareness that India has large and rapidly growing cities with complex infrastructure needs","Basic knowledge that cities need services like roads, water supply, garbage collection, and street lights","Understanding of the concept of elected representatives and democratic governance"],introduction:{why_important:"India is rapidly urbanising \u2014 more than 50 crore people now live in cities and towns. Cities face unique governance challenges: dense populations, complex infrastructure, traffic management, waste disposal, and air quality. Urban local bodies govern these challenges at the local level. Understanding urban administration helps students know who is responsible for city services and how citizens can participate.",real_life_relevance:"When a road outside your house is dug up and not repaired, when garbage is not collected for days, or when a streetlight has been broken for weeks \u2014 these are the responsibilities of your city's Urban Local Body (ULB). Knowing which body to approach and how to complain is a practical civic skill for every urban resident.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka has multiple urban local bodies. The most prominent is the BBMP (Bruhat Bengaluru Mahanagara Palike) \u2014 the municipal corporation governing Bengaluru, India's third-largest city and IT capital. BBMP is one of India's largest municipal bodies, covering over 800 sq km and serving 1.2+ crore people. Karnataka also has city corporations in Mysuru, Mangaluru, Hubballi-Dharwad, Kalaburagi, Belagavi, Tumakuru, and Davanagere. Smaller cities have City Municipal Councils (CMC) and Town Municipal Councils (TMC). Karnataka's 74th Amendment-based 'Karnataka Municipal Corporations Act' and 'Karnataka Municipalities Act' govern these bodies.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapters 25 and 26 covered rural administration \u2014 Panchayati Raj and the revenue/police system. Chapter 27 applies similar concepts to cities \u2014 showing how urban areas have a parallel system of elected local governance through municipal bodies."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Municipal Corporation",role:"The urban local body governing large cities (population typically over 5 lakh), responsible for all city services \u2014 roads, water, waste, parks, birth/death records"},{name:"Councillor (Ward Member)",role:"An elected representative of a ward (neighbourhood division) in the city, who represents residents' concerns in the municipal council"},{name:"Mayor",role:"The elected head of the Municipal Corporation \u2014 the city's highest elected official who chairs council meetings and represents the city"},{name:"Municipal Commissioner",role:"A senior IAS officer appointed by the state government who heads the municipal administration \u2014 the executive head who implements council decisions"},{name:"Citizens",role:"The urban residents who pay taxes, vote for councillors, participate in ward committees, and use municipal services"}],plot:["The chapter opens with the problem of a broken streetlight in a city neighbourhood \u2014 asking: who is responsible for fixing it?","It introduces Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) as the institutions responsible for governing cities and towns.","Three types of ULBs are introduced: Municipal Corporation (large cities), Municipal Council (medium towns), Town Panchayat (smaller towns).","The structure of a Municipal Corporation is explained: the city is divided into wards, each ward elects a councillor.","All councillors together form the Municipal Council, which elects a Mayor as its head.","The Municipal Commissioner (IAS officer) is introduced as the executive head who implements decisions.","The functions of Municipal Corporations are listed: water supply, roads, drainage, garbage collection, parks, street lights, birth/death certificates, primary health centres.","Sources of municipal income are explained: property tax, professional tax, water charges, government grants.","Ward committees are explained as sub-bodies within each ward that allow citizens to participate directly in local governance.","The 74th Constitutional Amendment (1992) \u2014 the urban counterpart of the 73rd Amendment \u2014 is introduced.","Karnataka's BBMP (Bengaluru) and other city corporations are used as real examples.","The chapter connects urban administration challenges (rapid growth, slums, traffic) to the need for strong ULBs."],moral:"Cities belong to their citizens. Urban local bodies exist to serve all residents \u2014 rich and poor. Active citizen participation, regular tax payment, and holding elected councillors accountable are essential for good city governance."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Types of Urban Local Bodies and their Structure",simple_explanation:"Cities and towns have different types of local bodies based on their size \u2014 Municipal Corporation for big cities, Municipal Council for medium towns, and Town Panchayat for small towns.",detailed_explanation:"Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) in India are classified by the size of the urban population they serve: (1) Municipal Corporation (Mahanagara Palike) \u2014 for large cities with population typically over 5 lakh. Examples: BBMP (Bengaluru), Mysuru City Corporation (MCC), Mangaluru City Corporation. Has a full-time Mayor (elected), a council of elected members, and a Municipal Commissioner (IAS officer). (2) City Municipal Council (CMC) / Municipal Council \u2014 for medium-sized cities (typically 50,000 to 5 lakh population). Has an elected council headed by a President. (3) Town Municipal Council (TMC) / Town Panchayat \u2014 for smaller towns (typically under 50,000 population). Structure common to all: City/town divided into WARDS. Each ward elects one COUNCILLOR. All councillors form the COUNCIL. Council elects the MAYOR (or President for smaller bodies). IAS officer (MUNICIPAL COMMISSIONER) heads the administration. The 74th Constitutional Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to ULBs \u2014 the urban equivalent of the 73rd Amendment for rural Panchayats.",real_life_example:"Bengaluru is divided into 243 wards. Each ward has approximately 30,000\u201350,000 residents. Each ward elects one councillor. All 243 councillors form the BBMP council. The council elects a Mayor. The BBMP Commissioner (IAS officer) manages the day-to-day administration. This structure means Bengaluru's governance involves both elected representatives (councillors, Mayor) and appointed officials (Commissioner) working together.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's urban hierarchy: BBMP governs Bengaluru \u2014 India's IT capital, third-largest city (1.2+ crore population, 800+ sq km area, 243 wards). City Corporations: Mysuru, Mangaluru, Hubballi-Dharwad, Kalaburagi, Belagavi, Tumakuru, Davanagere. Karnataka has over 300 Town Municipal Councils across the state for smaller towns. The 74th Amendment is implemented through the Karnataka Municipal Corporations Act (for corporations) and Karnataka Municipalities Act (for councils).",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse the Mayor (elected) with the Municipal Commissioner (appointed). Mayor = elected by councillors or directly by citizens \u2014 political head. Municipal Commissioner = IAS officer appointed by state government \u2014 administrative/executive head. The Mayor chairs meetings and represents the city; the Commissioner implements decisions and manages staff. Also: the 74th Amendment covers URBAN local bodies; the 73rd Amendment covers RURAL bodies. They are parallel but separate.",memory_tip:"ULB size pyramid: Small town = Town Panchayat. Medium town = Municipal Council. Large city = Municipal Corporation. Remember: 'Tiny-Medium-Massive' matches 'Panchayat-Council-Corporation.' As cities grow, so does the complexity of their local body."},{concept_name:"Functions and Finances of Urban Local Bodies",simple_explanation:"Municipal Corporations are responsible for all basic city services \u2014 roads, water, garbage, drains, parks, and street lights \u2014 and they pay for these using taxes collected from residents and grants from the government.",detailed_explanation:"Functions of Municipal Corporations: (1) Infrastructure \u2014 building and maintaining city roads, flyovers, bridges, footpaths. (2) Water Supply \u2014 operating water treatment plants, laying water pipes, providing drinking water. (3) Sanitation \u2014 garbage collection, managing landfills, building and maintaining public toilets, sewage treatment. (4) Health \u2014 running primary health centres, dispensaries, managing disease surveillance, issuing birth and death certificates. (5) Education \u2014 maintaining municipal schools and anganwadis. (6) Urban Planning \u2014 granting building permissions, regulating construction, maintaining parks and playgrounds. (7) Lighting \u2014 maintaining street lights. Sources of ULB Income: (1) Property Tax \u2014 the most important own tax; paid annually by owners of buildings and land in the city. (2) Professional Tax \u2014 paid by salaried employees and professionals. (3) Fees and Charges \u2014 building permission fees, water connection charges, parking fees. (4) State Government Grants \u2014 State Finance Commission allocations. (5) Central Government Grants \u2014 Smart Cities Mission, AMRUT (Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation).",real_life_example:"In Bengaluru, if your family owns a house or flat, you pay property tax to BBMP every year. This money funds road repairs, garbage collection trucks, park maintenance, and street light electricity bills. When BBMP digs up a road to lay new water pipes and fails to repair it, you can complain to your ward councillor or BBMP's online complaint portal. The money you paid as tax is supposed to fund the repair.",karnataka_example:"BBMP collects approximately \u20b93,000\u20134,000 crore in property tax annually \u2014 one of the largest municipal tax collections in India. BBMP's Palike Samparka Kendra (PSK) is a single-window service centre where citizens can pay taxes, get birth/death certificates, and complain about services. Karnataka's Smart Cities Mission covers Bengaluru, Mangaluru, Hubballi-Dharwad, Belagavi, Davanagere, and Shivamogga \u2014 providing central government funding for modern urban infrastructure. BBMP's Bengaluru ward boundaries are publicly available on the BBMP website \u2014 every resident can find their ward and ward councillor.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think property tax is a central government tax. Property tax is a LOCAL tax collected by the municipal body \u2014 it goes entirely to the city corporation, not to the state or central government. Also, students confuse 'professional tax' with 'income tax.' Professional tax is a small state-level tax on salaried employees/professionals collected by the ULB; income tax is a central government tax on earnings. They are completely different taxes.",memory_tip:"ULB functions = WISH+EP: Water supply, Infrastructure (roads), Sanitation (garbage), Health, Education, Planning (building permissions) + Lights (street). Income = Property tax + Professional tax + Fees + Grants. Remember: PAY PROPERTY TAX \u2192 GET CITY SERVICES. The direct link between citizen tax payment and city services."},{concept_name:"Citizens and Ward Committees",simple_explanation:"Ward committees allow residents to participate directly in their neighbourhood's governance \u2014 just as the Gram Sabha allows villagers to participate in Panchayat decisions. Active citizens make better cities.",detailed_explanation:"The 74th Constitutional Amendment mandated Ward Committees in Municipal Corporations in cities with population over 3 lakh. A ward committee is a sub-body within each ward that includes: the elected councillor (who chairs it), representatives of residents' associations, NGOs, and other civil society groups, and citizens who participate voluntarily. Ward committees allow citizens to: discuss neighbourhood priorities (which roads to repair, where to add footpaths, where to place garbage bins); review how the ward's budget was spent; flag maintenance problems to the councillor and municipal administration; participate in planning decisions (new construction, traffic changes). Beyond ward committees, urban citizens can: file complaints on ULB websites and helplines; attend public hearings on development plans; file RTI (Right to Information) applications about municipal expenditure; vote for their ward councillor every 5 years.",real_life_example:"In your neighbourhood, if a park is poorly maintained or a footpath is encroached by hawkers, you can attend your ward committee meeting and raise it formally. The councillor must respond. If many residents raise the same issue, the councillor is more likely to act \u2014 and if they do not, this becomes an issue in the next election. Ward committees are the urban equivalent of the Gram Sabha \u2014 a forum for direct civic participation.",karnataka_example:"BBMP's 243 wards each have a Ward Committee as mandated by the Karnataka Municipal Corporations Act. BBMP runs the 'Bengaluru Bruhat Namma BBMP' app and website where citizens can file complaints about potholes, garbage, broken streetlights, and waterlogging. Karnataka's Transparency Act requires BBMP to publish its budget, expenditure, and project details online. Janaagraha, a Bengaluru-based NGO, works with citizens and BBMP to improve ward-level governance and has developed 'I Change My City' \u2014 a platform for citizen engagement in urban governance issues.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think Ward Committees are the same as the Ward Councillor. The Ward Councillor is one elected person. The Ward Committee is a group that includes the councillor AND residents' representatives \u2014 a broader consultative body. Also, students may think only the Mayor can solve city problems. In practice, the ward councillor is the most accessible point of contact for neighbourhood-level issues \u2014 not the Mayor.",memory_tip:"Ward Committee = Gram Sabha in the city. Both are forums where ordinary citizens participate directly in their local governance. Rural = Gram Sabha (village meeting). Urban = Ward Committee (neighbourhood meeting). Same democratic principle, different setting."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Urban Local Body (ULB)",meaning:"The elected local government institution that governs cities and towns. Types include Municipal Corporation (large cities), Municipal Council (medium towns), and Town Panchayat (small towns). Established by the 74th Constitutional Amendment (1992)."},{term:"Municipal Corporation",meaning:"The urban local body governing large cities (typically population over 5 lakh). It has an elected Mayor, elected councillors, and an appointed Municipal Commissioner (IAS officer). Example: BBMP (Bengaluru), Mysuru City Corporation."},{term:"Ward (Urban)",meaning:"A small electoral division of a city or town. Each ward elects one councillor. A large city like Bengaluru has 243 wards; each ward has 30,000\u201350,000 residents."},{term:"Councillor",meaning:"An elected representative of a ward in the urban local body. Councillors collectively form the council that governs the city. The councillor is the most accessible elected official for neighbourhood-level issues."},{term:"Mayor",meaning:"The elected head of a Municipal Corporation \u2014 the city's highest elected official. The Mayor chairs council meetings, represents the city at official events, and provides political leadership. In Karnataka, BBMP's Mayor is elected by the councillors."},{term:"Municipal Commissioner",meaning:"A senior IAS officer appointed by the state government to head the municipal administration. The executive head who implements council decisions, manages the municipal staff, and runs day-to-day city services."},{term:"Property Tax",meaning:"An annual tax paid by owners of buildings and land within the city to the municipal body. The most important own source of revenue for Municipal Corporations. Used to fund city services like roads, garbage collection, and water supply."},{term:"74th Constitutional Amendment",meaning:"The 1992 amendment that gave constitutional status to Urban Local Bodies \u2014 the urban equivalent of the 73rd Amendment for rural Panchayats. Mandated elections, reserved seats for women and SC/ST, and created Ward Committees in large cities."},{term:"BBMP (Bruhat Bengaluru Mahanagara Palike)",meaning:"Karnataka's largest Municipal Corporation governing Bengaluru \u2014 India's IT capital and third-largest city. Covers 800+ sq km, serves 1.2+ crore residents, and has 243 wards. One of India's largest and most complex urban local bodies."},{term:"Ward Committee",meaning:"A sub-body within each ward in large Municipal Corporations (mandated for cities over 3 lakh population under the 74th Amendment). Includes the councillor and representatives of residents, NGOs, and civil society. Allows citizens to participate directly in neighbourhood governance \u2014 the urban equivalent of the Gram Sabha."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Comprehension",question:"Explain how a large city like Bengaluru is governed through its Municipal Corporation (BBMP).",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Ward division \u2014 Bengaluru is divided into 243 wards. Each ward has approximately 30,000\u201350,000 residents.","Step 2: Councillor election \u2014 Every 5 years, residents of each ward vote to elect one councillor. So 243 councillors are elected.","Step 3: Council and Mayor \u2014 All 243 councillors together form the BBMP Council. The council elects a Mayor from among themselves (or through direct election \u2014 varies by state rules).","Step 4: Municipal Commissioner \u2014 The state government appoints a senior IAS officer as the BBMP Commissioner. The Commissioner heads the administration and implements council decisions.","Step 5: Daily governance \u2014 The Commissioner and municipal staff manage day-to-day services: garbage collection, road repairs, water supply, park maintenance, street lights, building permissions.","Conclusion: BBMP governance = elected councillors (political) + appointed commissioner (administrative). Citizens participate through ward committees, complaints on the BBMP app, and voting every 5 years."]},{example_number:2,type:"Vocabulary in context",question:"Use the term 'property tax' in a sentence and explain why it is important for urban local bodies.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Recall meaning \u2014 Property tax is an annual tax paid by owners of buildings and land within the city to the Municipal Corporation.","Step 2: Connect to ULB finances \u2014 Property tax is the most important own source of revenue for Municipal Corporations. Without it, the corporation cannot fund roads, garbage trucks, park maintenance, or street light electricity.","Step 3: Write sentence: 'When Arjun's family registered their new flat in Bengaluru, the first annual bill they received was from BBMP \u2014 a property tax of \u20b98,000 \u2014 which they understood as their contribution to keeping their neighbourhood's roads repaired and garbage collected throughout the year.'"]},{example_number:3,type:"Moral application",question:"The street outside your house in Bengaluru has been flooded for three days after a heavy rain. Garbage is not being collected. What steps should a responsible citizen take?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Identify responsibility \u2014 Drainage maintenance and garbage collection are BBMP's responsibility.","Step 2: File a complaint \u2014 Use BBMP's official complaint channels: the BBMP website, the 'Bengaluru Bruhat Namma BBMP' app, or the BBMP helpline. File separate complaints for flooding (drainage issue) and garbage collection failure.","Step 3: Contact the Ward Councillor \u2014 Approach your elected ward councillor directly. They are responsible for the ward and must take up maintenance issues with the BBMP administration.","Step 4: Ward Committee \u2014 If the issue persists, raise it at the Ward Committee meeting where the councillor chairs and other residents can collectively demand action.","Step 5: Escalate \u2014 If BBMP does not respond within reasonable time, file an RTI (Right to Information) request asking what steps were taken and when.","Conclusion: Citizens are not powerless. BBMP's complaint system, ward councillors, ward committees, and RTI are all tools for citizens to hold their city administration accountable."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Types of Urban Local Bodies and their Structure",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What is the full form of BBMP?",a:"BBMP stands for Bruhat Bengaluru Mahanagara Palike \u2014 the Municipal Corporation governing Bengaluru."},{type:"Recall",q:"Which Constitutional Amendment gave constitutional status to Urban Local Bodies?",a:"The 74th Constitutional Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to Urban Local Bodies \u2014 mandating elections, reservations, and ward committees."},{type:"Understanding",q:"How is the Mayor different from the Municipal Commissioner in a city corporation?",a:"The Mayor is an ELECTED representative \u2014 chosen by ward councillors or by citizens. The Mayor is the political head who chairs council meetings and represents the city. The Municipal Commissioner is an APPOINTED IAS officer \u2014 chosen by the state government. The Commissioner is the administrative/executive head who manages daily operations and implements council decisions."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why is the Ward Committee called the 'urban equivalent of the Gram Sabha'?",a:"Both allow ordinary citizens to participate directly in their local governance beyond just voting. The Gram Sabha is a forum for all rural adult voters to discuss and review village governance. The Ward Committee is a forum for urban residents to discuss and review neighbourhood governance. Both represent direct citizen participation \u2014 not just representative democracy."},{type:"Application",q:"A new resident moves into a Bengaluru apartment. How do they find out who their ward councillor is and how to contact BBMP for services?",a:"They can: visit the BBMP website (bbmp.gov.in) and use the 'Find Your Ward' feature with their address; download the BBMP mobile app which shows ward details and allows complaints; visit the nearest BBMP office (Palike Samparka Kendra); or ask a neighbour or residents' association. Knowing your ward councillor is the first step to participating in urban governance."}]}],key_takeaways:["Urban Local Bodies (ULBs) govern India's cities and towns \u2014 the urban equivalent of Panchayati Raj.","Three types: Municipal Corporation (large cities), Municipal Council (medium towns), Town Panchayat (small towns).","City is divided into wards; each ward elects a councillor; all councillors form the council; council elects the Mayor.","Municipal Commissioner = IAS officer appointed by state government \u2014 executive head who implements decisions.","Mayor = elected head of Municipal Corporation \u2014 political representative.","74th Constitutional Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to ULBs \u2014 parallel to 73rd Amendment for rural bodies.","ULB functions: water supply, roads, garbage, sanitation, health, education, urban planning, street lights, birth/death records.","ULB income: property tax (most important), professional tax, fees, state and central government grants.","BBMP is Karnataka's largest municipal body: 800+ sq km, 1.2+ crore people, 243 wards.","Ward Committees allow citizens to participate directly \u2014 the urban equivalent of the Gram Sabha."],mind_map:{root:"Urban Administration",branches:[{branch:"Types of ULBs",sub:["Municipal Corporation \u2014 large cities (5 lakh+)","Municipal Council \u2014 medium towns","Town Panchayat \u2014 small towns","74th Amendment \u2014 constitutional status"]},{branch:"Structure of Municipal Corporation",sub:["City \u2192 Wards \u2192 Councillors (elected)","All councillors = Council","Mayor = elected head","Municipal Commissioner = IAS officer (executive)"]},{branch:"Functions",sub:["Roads, bridges, footpaths","Water supply and sewage","Garbage collection, sanitation","Health, education, parks, street lights","Building permissions, birth/death certificates"]},{branch:"Finances",sub:["Property tax (most important own revenue)","Professional tax, fees, charges","State government grants","Central grants: Smart Cities, AMRUT"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["BBMP \u2014 Bengaluru \u2014 243 wards, 800 sq km","Mysuru, Mangaluru, Hubballi-Dharwad corporations","BBMP app \u2014 citizen complaints","Janaagraha \u2014 civic engagement NGO, Bengaluru"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 27 covers Urban Administration \u2014 the governance of India's rapidly growing cities through Urban Local Bodies (ULBs). Three types of ULBs: Municipal Corporation (large cities, 5 lakh+ population), Municipal Council (medium towns), and Town Panchayat (small towns). Structure: city divided into wards; each ward elects a councillor; all councillors form the municipal council; council elects the Mayor; a Municipal Commissioner (IAS officer) is appointed by the state government as the executive head. The 74th Constitutional Amendment (1992) gave constitutional status to ULBs \u2014 reserving seats for women and SC/ST, mandating elections every 5 years, and requiring Ward Committees in large cities. ULB functions: roads, water supply, garbage, sanitation, health, education, parks, street lights, building permissions. Income: property tax (most important own source), professional tax, fees, state and central grants. BBMP (Bruhat Bengaluru Mahanagara Palike) governs Bengaluru \u2014 800+ sq km, 1.2 crore population, 243 wards. Karnataka also has city corporations in Mysuru, Mangaluru, Hubballi-Dharwad, Kalaburagi, Belagavi. Ward Committees allow direct citizen participation in neighbourhood governance \u2014 the urban Gram Sabha. Key vocabulary: ward, councillor, Mayor, Municipal Commissioner, property tax, 74th Amendment, BBMP, Ward Committee.",must_remember_facts:["Three types of ULBs: Municipal Corporation (large), Municipal Council (medium), Town Panchayat (small).","74th Constitutional Amendment (1992) \u2014 gives constitutional status to ULBs; parallel to 73rd Amendment for rural bodies.","City \u2192 Wards \u2192 Councillors \u2192 Council \u2192 Mayor (elected chain).","Municipal Commissioner = IAS officer appointed by state government \u2014 executive head (NOT elected).","Mayor = elected head of Municipal Corporation \u2014 political representative.","ULB functions: roads, water, garbage, sanitation, health, education, urban planning, street lights.","Property tax = most important own revenue source for Municipal Corporations.","BBMP = Bruhat Bengaluru Mahanagara Palike \u2014 governs Bengaluru, 243 wards, 800+ sq km.","Ward Committees = urban equivalent of Gram Sabha \u2014 direct citizen participation in neighbourhood governance.","Karnataka City Corporations: Bengaluru (BBMP), Mysuru, Mangaluru, Hubballi-Dharwad, Kalaburagi, Belagavi, Tumakuru, Davanagere."]}},2567,[]);